// EXPECT: E0256 — a `#foreign` procedure installed into a procedure-pointer field (ADR-0062 §3)
//
// `context.allocator = malloc` is refused, and the *reason it gets its own diagnostic* is what this
// file is really about. A `#foreign` procedure's type is `ContextKind::CCall` (ADR-0001) and a
// procedure-pointer type is always `Jairs` (ADR-0059 §3), so the two are different interned types and
// an ordinary type mismatch would report:
//
//     expected `(s64) -> *u8`, found `(s64) -> *u8`
//
// — identical text, because the difference is invisible. That is not a message a reader can act on,
// and it is not something they could change even if they saw it: the C convention is the whole point
// of `#foreign`. So this reuses E0256, the code ADR-0059 §5 defined for taking a `#foreign` procedure
// as a value, because it is the same objection reached through assignment.
//
// The actionable answer is the wrapper, which `valid/050-allocator.jr` shows:
//
//     libc_alloc :: (n: s64) -> *u8 { return malloc(n); }
//     context.allocator = libc_alloc;
//
// An **imported** `#foreign` procedure is used deliberately. A same-file one already reported E0256
// before this wave; the imported case fell through to the two-identical-types mismatch, because the
// check only looked at this file's HIR. It now asks the *type* instead, which is the thing that
// crosses the module boundary.
//
// **Filed under `imports/invalid/` rather than `type-errors/`** for exactly that reason: it needs the
// `#import` resolved to reach the case at all, and `type-errors/` files are checked with modules
// *unresolved* on purpose (so sema stays silent about poison rather than inventing type errors). A
// same-file version of this would test the path that already worked.
#import "Basic";

install_foreign :: () {
    // The refusal: libc's `malloc`, straight into the context's allocator field.
    context.allocator = malloc;
}
